x86_64-windows  a `try` inside a `catch` handler is refused for this target: a Microsoft handler is a funclet named `<fn>$catch$N` from a per-function counter, so a nested one takes a name already used (try-in-handler.notarget). The jumps this case measures - `return`, `break` and `continue` out of a handler - compile for this target since 2026-09-26 (handler-exit-ms.cpp), and the class-typed throw since the same day; what they do here is leave the funclet and carry on in the parent, there being no `__cxa_end_catch` on this ABI to call.
